{"title":"2023 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 23","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Linearity_of_Expectation)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Linearity of Expectation)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Simple_and_Quick)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Simple and Quick)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Math-X_(Smart_and_Simple)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Math-X (Smart and Simple)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(A_Clever_Explanation_You’ll_Get_Instantly)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (A Clever Explanation You’ll Get Instantly)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(THINKING_CREATIVELY!!!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (THINKING CREATIVELY!!!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Animated_Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Animated Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Magic_Square\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Magic Square</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Interstigation\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Interstigation</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_WhyMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">14</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by WhyMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-15\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_harungurcan\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">15</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by harungurcan</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-16\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">16</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-17\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Dr._David\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">17</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Dr. David</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-18\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_SquishBJ_(AIME_Qualifier)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">18</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by SquishBJ (AIME Qualifier)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-19\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">19</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Each square in a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/7/d278803d72fcc493afb1559174a483aa7f41d143.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> grid is randomly filled with one of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> gray and white tiles shown below on the right. \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/1/c01163352203e89b9926215ea00f34b275a6c630.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(5.663333333cm); draw((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(0,3)--cycle,gray); draw((1,0)--(1,3)--(2,3)--(2,0),gray); draw((0,1)--(3,1)--(3,2)--(0,2),gray);  fill((6,.33)--(7,.33)--(7,1.33)--cycle,mediumgray); draw((6,.33)--(7,.33)--(7,1.33)--(6,1.33)--cycle,gray); fill((6,1.67)--(7,2.67)--(6,2.67)--cycle,mediumgray); draw((6,1.67)--(7,1.67)--(7,2.67)--(6,2.67)--cycle,gray); fill((7.33,.33)--(8.33,.33)--(7.33,1.33)--cycle,mediumgray); draw((7.33,.33)--(8.33,.33)--(8.33,1.33)--(7.33,1.33)--cycle,gray); fill((8.33,1.67)--(8.33,2.67)--(7.33,2.67)--cycle,mediumgray); draw((7.33,1.67)--(8.33,1.67)--(8.33,2.67)--(7.33,2.67)--cycle,gray); [/asy]\" width=\"268\" height=\"98\" />\nWhat is the probability that the tiling will contain a large gray diamond in one of the smaller <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/c/e4c044326e2e84b768dafef07060bd42b0a5cb21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\times 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> grids? Below is an example of such tiling. \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/9/8d9055c9c5a4feb6f71e870557f61ce1d6b5033a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(2cm);  fill((1,0)--(0,1)--(0,2)--(1,1)--cycle,mediumgray); fill((2,0)--(3,1)--(2,2)--(1,1)--cycle,mediumgray); fill((1,2)--(1,3)--(0,3)--cycle,mediumgray); fill((1,2)--(2,2)--(2,3)--cycle,mediumgray); fill((3,2)--(3,3)--(2,3)--cycle,mediumgray);  draw((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(0,3)--cycle,gray); draw((1,0)--(1,3)--(2,3)--(2,0),gray); draw((0,1)--(3,1)--(3,2)--(0,2),gray); [/asy]\" width=\"95\" height=\"95\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/acc04643e80ab26da451b0c410ac5c7f8ffd6bad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } \\frac{1}{1024} \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } \\frac{1}{256} \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } \\frac{1}{64} \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } \\frac{1}{16} \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"439\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cases that the tiling will contain a large gray diamond in one of the smaller <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/c/e4c044326e2e84b768dafef07060bd42b0a5cb21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\times 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> grids, as shown below:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/74446ea97a934723e3120b4ce500a623faa87d9c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(375);  fill((1,1)--(2,2)--(1,3)--(0,2)--cycle,mediumgray); draw((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(0,3)--cycle,gray); draw((1,0)--(1,3)--(2,3)--(2,0),gray); draw((0,1)--(3,1)--(3,2)--(0,2),gray);  fill(shift(7,0)*((1,1)--(2,2)--(1,3)--(0,2)--cycle),mediumgray); draw(shift(6,0)*((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(0,3)--cycle),gray); draw(shift(6,0)*((1,0)--(1,3)--(2,3)--(2,0)),gray); draw(shift(6,0)*((0,1)--(3,1)--(3,2)--(0,2)),gray);  fill(shift(12,-1)*((1,1)--(2,2)--(1,3)--(0,2)--cycle),mediumgray); draw(shift(12,0)*((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(0,3)--cycle),gray); draw(shift(12,0)*((1,0)--(1,3)--(2,3)--(2,0)),gray); draw(shift(12,0)*((0,1)--(3,1)--(3,2)--(0,2)),gray);  fill(shift(19,-1)*((1,1)--(2,2)--(1,3)--(0,2)--cycle),mediumgray); draw(shift(18,0)*((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(0,3)--cycle),gray); draw(shift(18,0)*((1,0)--(1,3)--(2,3)--(2,0)),gray); draw(shift(18,0)*((0,1)--(3,1)--(3,2)--(0,2)),gray); [/asy]\" width=\"625\" height=\"92\" />\nThere are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/81857af0dfe8c24f28ddc0a500259da537b890d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4^5$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" /> ways to decide the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> white squares for each case, and the cases do not have any overlap.\n</p><p>So, the requested probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/c/1ccad1af2529ea4ae5bad082ce11ce24d65a3f04.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{4\\cdot4^5}{4^9} = \\frac{4^6}{4^9} = \\frac{1}{4^3} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } \\frac{1}{64}}.\\]\" width=\"226\" height=\"48\" />\n~apex304, TaeKim, MRENTHUSIASM\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that the middle tile can be any of the four tiles. The gray part of the middle tile points towards one of the corners, and for the gray diamond to appear the three adjacent tiles must all be perfect. Thus, the solution is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/b/86b0a59bf6c84d75563f9eee8bd61d111e2400e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac14 \\cdot \\frac14 \\cdot \\frac14 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } \\frac{1}{64}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"160\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~aayr\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that each tile must be in its precise place. Because of that, each diamond has a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/5/77530eb6099b4813d32c9910aacea153587ee6e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac14\\right)^4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"45\" height=\"46\" /> chance of appearing. And since there are 4 placements, our solution is<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/0/d00b39350deaa5fb840b698c4bd9a9758e69e8a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4^4} \\cdot 4 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } \\frac{1}{64}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"137\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~ligonmathkid2\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Linearity_of_Expectation.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Linearity_of_Expectation)\">Solution 4 (Linearity of Expectation)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Linearity of Expectation)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/d/4fdff1fce72d4e269b5c7e44cdda5d9c483f8006.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_1, S_2, S_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"70\" height=\"16\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/7/927ca6f264730b7f22ea5e06ee7ac6872819f94e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" /> denote the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> smaller <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/c/e4c044326e2e84b768dafef07060bd42b0a5cb21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\times 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> squares within the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/7/d278803d72fcc493afb1559174a483aa7f41d143.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> square in some order. For each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/0/9e0ec34cd8860ceb97113f81ba768ba739ad5166.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" />, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/6/c061d6ae18e3c275cea6ef11d699788794ade4a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X_i = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" /> if it contains a large gray diamond tiling and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/3/5b3eb69f95350d83fde53455ca268caac8b39e89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X_i = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" /> otherwise. This means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/5/4a5abedf8107b2fa9f5714fba2748f1ebeff4a3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{E}[X_i]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> is the probability that square <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/0/9e0ec34cd8860ceb97113f81ba768ba739ad5166.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" /> has a large gray diamond, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/e/4deedad79bd3c137d4c8b1fe7296c06975df3a25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{E}[X_1 + X_2 + X_3 + X_4]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"180\" height=\"20\" /> is our desired probability. However, since there is only one possible way to arrange the squares within every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/c/e4c044326e2e84b768dafef07060bd42b0a5cb21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\times 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> square to form such a tiling, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/d/b5d8029da7d4710f45e0763c4b97096b8c55ff2c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{E}[X_i] = (\\tfrac{1}{4})^4 = \\tfrac{1}{256}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"149\" height=\"23\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" /> (as each of the smallest tiles has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> possible arrangements), and from the linearity of expectation we get \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/8/a3878da5d0d4fac3810f9880d2e841af23dc020e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\mathbb{E}[X_1 + X_2 + X_3 + X_4] = \\mathbb{E}[X_1] + \\mathbb{E}[X_2] + \\mathbb{E}[X_3] + \\mathbb{E}[X_4] = \\frac{1}{256} + \\frac{1}{256} + \\frac{1}{256} + \\frac{1}{256} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } \\frac{1}{64}}.\\]\" width=\"686\" height=\"48\" />\n~eibc\n</p><p>Remark 1: This method might be too advanced for the AMC 8, and is probably unnecessary (refer to the other solutions for simpler techniques).\n</p><p>Remark 2: Note that Probability and Expected Value are equivalent in this problem since there will never be two diamonds on one tiling. i.e. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/d/07d317eb3605abdd7af230e427b08c665063a63b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X_1 + X_2 + X_3 + X_4 \\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"192\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>~numerophile\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Simple_and_Quick.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Simple_and_Quick)\">Solution 5 (Simple and Quick)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Simple and Quick)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can start by finding the probability of making a diamond with the squares in ONLY the top-left corner of the square. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/4/2b4a5009689eb881c2732d852b14d444cb0f4044.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4\\times 4\\times 4\\times 4 = 256$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"156\" height=\"15\" /> total cases of putting four random tiles in the top-left corner. Since there is only one favorable case (the case of making a diamond) out of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/d/abd678361b0f40ec2723283c0f2bab3df5cadc68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$256$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> total, the probability of making a diamond is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/c/ecc4ea0265711e669f078fd562840f44b21c621f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{256}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"38\" height=\"38\" /> Now, we can simply multiply this by four to account for all corners of the square to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/d/89d8bd9b146518dfc70f038bc8cc1c43e737d14e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4\\times \\frac{1}{256}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"64\" height=\"38\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/8/6a8cfce4112490b9b1e3af0bc95adc1b6c26c9b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } \\frac{1}{64}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"75\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~mathguy567\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_Math-X_.28Smart_and_Simple.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Math-X_(Smart_and_Simple)\">Video Solution by Math-X (Smart and Simple)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Math-X (Smart and Simple)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/Ku_c1YHnLt0?si=wz7F8E_c7Hxv9GQ5&amp;t=5137\">https://youtu.be/Ku_c1YHnLt0?si=wz7F8E_c7Hxv9GQ5&amp;t=5137</a> \n</p><p>~Math-X\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.28A_Clever_Explanation_You.E2.80.99ll_Get_Instantly.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_(A_Clever_Explanation_You’ll_Get_Instantly)\">Video Solution (A Clever Explanation You’ll Get Instantly)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution (A Clever Explanation You’ll Get Instantly)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/zntZrtsnyxc?si=nM5eWOwNU6HRdleZ&amp;t=2831\">https://youtu.be/zntZrtsnyxc?si=nM5eWOwNU6HRdleZ&amp;t=2831</a>\n~hsnacademy\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.28THINKING_CREATIVELY.21.21.21.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_(THINKING_CREATIVELY!!!)\">Video Solution (THINKING CREATIVELY!!!)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.ph